{"reference":"Matthew 13","book":"Matthew","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Parables as a Collection","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew 13 gathers parables about the kingdom amid changing audiences and selective explanations. Its images of sowing, fields, and fishing resonate with an unequal agrarian Roman-Palestinian economy. The formula in verse 53, “when Jesus had finished these parables,” is one of five repeated discourse-ending formulas that organize major teaching collections in Matthew. This pattern may help readers notice the chapter’s place within a larger arrangement, but it does not prove a universally accepted symbolic outline for the book.","verse_refs":["Matthew 13:1-33","Matthew 13:1-53","Matthew 13:53"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_013_424c930693289ec758d2e465"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Cautious Comparison","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"At verse 41, language and motifs in Matthew may be compared with Zephaniah, and similar comparisons can be made with Revelation. Each proposed connection needs to be considered passage by passage: direct quotation, use of a shared tradition, and broader prophetic reuse are different claims.","verse_refs":["Matthew 13:41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_013_479e16912e683f4bae20948a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}